OpenEmbedded/Yocto Linux BSP for NXP Auto platforms
===================================================


First time setup
----------------

To obtain the BSP, repo and its prerequisites must be installed.
This only needs to be done once.


Install dependencies:
- python 3.6.0 or newer ($: sudo <pkg-mgr> install python3)
- git 1.8.3 or newer ($: sudo <pkg-mgr> install git)
- curl ($: sudo <pkg-mgr> install curl)
where <pkg-mgr> is the package manager for the host distribution.
(apt-get for Debian/Ubuntu, yum or dnf for CentOS/Fedora, zypper for Suse).

Install the `repo` utility:

$: mkdir ~/bin
$: curl https://storage.googleapis.com/git-repo-downloads/repo > ~/bin/repo
$: chmod a+x ~/bin/repo
$: PATH=${PATH}:~/bin


Download the Yocto Project Environment into a directory:
-----------------------------------------------------------

$: mkdir fsl-auto-yocto-bsp
$: cd fsl-auto-yocto-bsp
$: repo init -u https://github.com/nxp-auto-linux/auto_yocto_bsp -b release/<prefix><major>.<minor>
$: repo sync

Where <prefix> should be set to "bsp" for G releases and to "s32n7_" for N releases.

This downloads the sources for the latest NXP Auto Linux BSP from the `release/<prefix><major>.<minor>` branch,
based on the Yocto Scarthgap release.

Note: To use a different Linux BSP release or an engineering build, select the corresponding branch.


Creating a Build Directory for a BSP release:
---------------------------------------------

To install all prerequisites before starting any build, run:
$ ./sources/meta-alb/scripts/host-prepare.sh

Create build directory in the BSP root by running the following command:

$ source nxp-setup-alb.sh -m <machine>

where:
<machine>	is any NXP Auto target, e.g. s32n79rdb, s32g399aevb3, s32r45evb, s32g399ardb3ubuntu etc.


Build an image:
---------------

In the build directory created by the setup script, run the following command:

$ bitbake <image-name>

For the NXP Auto BSP, `<image-name>` may be any of the Auto images, such as
`fsl-image-base`, `fsl-image-auto`, `fsl-image-ubuntu`, or `fsl-image-ubuntu-base`.
Ubuntu images can only be built for ubuntu machines, while yocto images can
only be built for non-Ubuntu machines.

Running the above command builds U-Boot, the kernel, kernel modules,
and a deployable root filesystem. The generated build artifacts can be found in:
`build_<machine>/tmp/deploy/images/`

The `<image-name>.sdcard` file is a disk image containing all required partitions,
including the bootloader, kernel, and root filesystem. The contents of this file can
be written directly to an SD card device using `dd`, as shown in the following example:

$ sudo dd if=<image-name>.sdcard of=/dev/sd<partition> bs=1M && sync

Ensure that any partitions on the card are properly unmounted before writing
the card image; otherwise, the resulting card image may be corrupted.
Also ensure that the filesystem is properly synchronized before ejecting
the card to guarantee that all data has been written.

To deploy only the rootfs to the target partition, run:
$ sudo tar -xzf <image-name>.tar.gz -C <destination>
where <destination> is the destination partition (as mounted on the host).

Note that BitBake builds accumulate in the deployment directory.
Older, no longer relevant images may be removed after repeated builds.
The first build can take a significant amount of time because extensive
one-time setup and build tasks must be completed.
A sufficiently powerful build machine is recommended.

When resuming Yocto builds in a new shell, source the `SOURCE_THIS` file before continuing.


Images provided
===============

fsl-image-ubuntu-base: A system image with a minimalist Ubuntu 24.04 LTS root filesystem

fsl-image-ubuntu: A complete, development-suitable system image based on Ubuntu 24.04 LTS
which contains (among others): the complete kernel source code (for rebuilding the kernel directly on the target machine),
compilers and toolchains (for C, C++, Java), ROS Kinetic and Docker

NOTE: Please build fsl-image-ubuntu-base or fsl-image-ubuntu only for machines with name ending in `ubuntu`.
For the other machines, please use any of the images with Yocto rootfilesystem.

NOTE: For the most convenient development environment, use of
fsl-image-auto below is suggested.
Production embedded projects would likely use much smaller images.

fsl-image-auto: A complete system image (containing TF-A, U-Boot, Linux kernel and DTBs) with a full Yocto-sourced filesystem.
fsl-image-base: A minimal image (containing TF-A, U-Boot, Linux kernel and DTBs) with Yocto-sourced filesystem.
---------------

The complete root filesystem provides a reasonably complete system and
includes the essential tools required for remote access and for
mounting NFS and other storage devices.